  ("ing the output as needed. In this tutorial, we describe the self-adjusting-" 0.16315 0.83497 0.36027 0.37247 0.37247 0.0122 0.00637 0.2590 0.0286 "CMR9" 0.0136 nil nil 11 "CMR9" nil)
  ("computation model and present the language ΔML (Delta ML) for" 0.16315 0.8354 0.37536 0.38901 0.38901 0.0122 0.01232 0.2709 0.1177 "CMR9" 0.0136 nil nil 9 "CMR9" nil)
  ("writing self-adjusting programs." 0.16315 0.46087 0.39354 0.40574 0.40574 0.0122 0.00742 0.2953 0.0665 "CMR9" 0.0136 nil nil 2 "CMR9" nil)
  ("1" 0.09729 0.11294 0.44095 0.45712 0.45712 0.01618 nil nil 0.0157 "CMBX12" 0.0181 t nil 0 "CMBX12" t)
  ("Introduction" 0.14419 0.31655 0.44095 0.45712 0.45712 0.01618 nil 0.1898 0.1724 "CMBX12" 0.0181 t nil 0 "CMBX12" t)
  ("Since the early years of computer science, researchers realized that many uses of" 0.09729 0.90172 0.47886 0.49241 0.49241 0.01356 0.00675 0.2589 0.0528 "CMR10" 0.0151 nil nil 12 "CMR10" nil)
  ("computer applications are incremental by nature. We start an application with" 0.09729 0.9014 0.49704 0.51059 0.51059 0.01356 0.00795 0.2878 0.0954 "CMR10" 0.0151 nil nil 10 "CMR10" nil)
  ("some initial input to obtain some initial output. We then observe the output," 0.09729 0.9016 0.51504 0.52859 0.52859 0.01356 0.00945 0.2729 0.0504 "CMR10" 0.0151 nil nil 12 "CMR10" nil)
  ("make some small modifications to the input and re-compute the output. We" 0.09729 0.90147 0.53322 0.54678 0.54678 0.01356 0.01063 0.2783 0.0530 "CMR10" 0.0151 nil nil 11 "CMR10" nil)
  ("often repeat this process of modifying the input incrementally and re-computing" 0.09729 0.90201 0.5514 0.56496 0.56496 0.01356 0.00655 0.2956 0.0509 "CMR10" 0.0151 nil nil 10 "CMR10" nil)
  ("the output. In many applications, incremental modifications to the input cause" 0.09729 0.90192 0.5694 0.58296 0.58296 0.01356 0.00806 0.2724 0.0321 "CMR10" 0.0151 nil nil 10 "CMR10" nil)
  ("only incremental modifications to the output, raising the question of whether it" 0.09729 0.90162 0.58759 0.60114 0.60114 0.01356 0.00739 0.2743 0.0432 "CMR10" 0.0151 nil nil 11 "CMR10" nil)
  ("would be possible to update the output faster than recomputing from scratch." 0.09729 0.89088 0.60559 0.61914 0.61914 0.01356 0.00773 0.2519 0.0601 "CMR10" 0.0151 nil nil 11 "CMR10" nil)
  ("Examples of this phenomena abound. For example, applications that interact" 0.12352 0.90186 0.62377 0.63732 0.63732 0.01356 0.00687 0.2896 0.0979 "CMR10" 0.0151 nil nil 9 "CMR10" nil)
  ("with or model the physical world (e.g., robots, traffic control systems, schedul-" 0.09729 0.90162 0.64195 0.6555 0.6555 0.01356 0.00812 0.2877 0.0450 "CMR10" 0.0151 nil nil 11 "CMR10" nil)
  ("ing systems) observe the world evolve" 0.09729 0.48884 0.65995 0.67351 0.67351 0.01356 0.00941 0.2321 0.0309 "CMR10" 0.0151 nil nil 5 "CMR10" nil)
  ("slowly over time and must respond to" 0.50781 0.90149 0.65995 0.67351 0.67351 0.01356 0.00946 0.2651 0.0622 "CMR10" 0.0151 nil nil 6 "CMR10" nil)
  ("those changes efficiently. Similarly in applications that interact with the user," 0.09729 0.90173 0.67813 0.69169 0.69169 0.01356 0.00952 0.2569 0.0530 "CMR10" 0.0151 nil nil 10 "CMR10" nil)
  ("application-data changes incrementally over time as a result of user commands." 0.09729 0.90151 0.69631 0.70987 0.70987 0.01356 0.00739 0.2858 0.1652 "CMR10" 0.0151 nil nil 10 "CMR10" nil)
  ("For example, in software development, the compiler is invoked repeatedly after" 0.09729 0.90147 0.71431 0.72787 0.72787 0.01356 0.00814 0.2763 0.0339 "CMR10" 0.0151 nil nil 10 "CMR10" nil)
  ("the user makes small changes to the program code. Other example applica-" 0.09729 0.9019 0.7325 0.74605 0.74605 0.01356 0.01143 0.2468 0.0321 "CMR10" 0.0151 nil nil 11 "CMR10" nil)
  ("tion areas include databases, scientific computing (e.g., physical simulations)," 0.09729 0.90164 0.75068 0.76423 0.76423 0.01356 0.01003 0.2525 0.0400 "CMR10" 0.0151 nil nil 8 "CMR10" nil)
  ("graphics, etc." 0.09729 0.23127 0.76868 0.78223 0.78223 0.01356 0.00724 0.2139 0.0907 "CMR10" 0.0151 nil nil 1 "CMR10" nil)
  ("In many of the aforementioned examples, modifications to the computation" 0.12352 0.9017 0.78686 0.80042 0.80042 0.01356 0.00911 0.2753 0.0212 "CMR10" 0.0151 nil nil 9 "CMR10" nil)
  ("data or input are external (e.g., the user modifies some data). In others, incre-" 0.09729 0.90191 0.80486 0.81842 0.81842 0.01356 0.00823 0.2477 0.0452 "CMR10" 0.0151 nil nil 13 "CMR10" nil)
  ("mental modifications are inherent. For example, in motion simulation, objects" 0.09729 0.90168 0.82304 0.8366 0.8366 0.01356 0.00919 0.2972 0.0689 "CMR10" 0.0151 nil nil 9 "CMR10" nil)
  ("move continuously over time causing the property being computed to change" 0.09729 0.90194 0.84122 0.85478 0.85478 0.01356 0.01001 0.2233 0.0525 "CMR10" 0.0151 nil nil 10 "CMR10" nil)
  ("continuously as well. In particular, if we wish to simulate the flow of a fluid by" 0.09729 0.90146 0.85923 0.87278 0.87278 0.01356 0.00806 0.2851 0.1271 "CMR10" 0.0151 nil nil 15 "CMR10" nil)
  ("modeling its constituent particles, then we need to compute certain properties" 0.09729 0.90182 0.87741 0.89096 0.89096 0.01356 0.00871 0.2457 0.0920 "CMR10" 0.0151 nil nil 10 "CMR10" nil)
  ("of moving objects, e.g., we may want to triangulate the particles to compute the" 0.09729 0.90122 0.89559 0.90914 0.90914 0.01356 0.00662 0.3095 0.0188 "CMR10" 0.0151 nil nil 13 "CMR10" nil)
  ("P. Koopman and D. Swierstra (Eds.): AFP 2008, LNCS 5832, pp. 1–38, 2009." 0.09729 0.72633 0.93181 0.94129 0.94129 0.00948 0.0062 0.2930 0.0164 "CMR7" 0.0106 nil nil 12 "CMR7" nil)
  ("c" 0.10231 0.11055 0.94363 0.95311 0.95311 0.00948 nil nil 0.0082 "CMR7" 0.0106 nil nil 0 "CMR7" nil)
  ("⃝ Springer-Verlag Berlin Heidelberg 2009" 0.09729 0.43853 0.94287 0.95347 0.95347 0.00948 0.00584 0.3887 0.1507 "CMR7" 0.0106 nil nil 3 "CMSY7" nil))
  (16
  ("2" 0.09729 0.10801 0.04354 0.05574 0.05574 0.0122 nil nil 0.0107 "CMR9" 0.0136 nil nil 0 "CMR9" nil)
  ("U.A. Acar and R. Ley-Wild" 0.17404 0.43478 0.04354 0.05574 0.05574 0.0122 0.00716 0.4138 0.0442 "CMR9" 0.0136 nil nil 4 "CMR9" nil)
  ("forces exerted between particles, and update those properties as the points move." 0.09729 0.90189 0.08158 0.09514 0.09514 0.01356 0.00583 0.2699 0.0576 "CMR10" 0.0151 nil nil 11 "CMR10" nil)
  ("Since the combinatorial structure of the computed properties change slowly over" 0.09729 0.90207 0.09977 0.11332 0.11332 0.01356 0.00645 0.2736 0.0527 "CMR10" 0.0151 nil nil 10 "CMR10" nil)
  ("time, we can often view continuous motion as an incremental modification; this" 0.09729 0.90162 0.11795 0.1315 0.1315 0.01356 0.0075 0.3075 0.0514 "CMR10" 0.0151 nil nil 11 "CMR10" nil)
  ("makes it possible to compute the output more efficiently than re-computing it" 0.09729 0.90146 0.13595 0.1495 0.1495 0.01356 0.00885 0.2783 0.0622 "CMR10" 0.0151 nil nil 11 "CMR10" nil)
  ("from-scratch at fixed intervals." 0.09729 0.40771 0.15413 0.16769 0.16769 0.01356 0.00773 0.2536 0.1270 "CMR10" 0.0151 nil nil 3 "CMR10" nil)
  ("Although incremental applications abound, no effective general-purpose tech-" 0.12352 0.90162 0.17231 0.18587 0.18587 0.01356 0.00673 0.2590 0.0948 "CMR10" 0.0151 nil nil 7 "CMR10" nil)
  ("nique or language was known for developing incremental applications until" 0.09729 0.90146 0.19031 0.20387 0.20387 0.01356 0.01282 0.2325 0.0547 "CMR10" 0.0151 nil nil 9 "CMR10" nil)
  ("recently (see Section 10 for the discussion of the earlier work on the subject)." 0.09729 0.9014 0.20849 0.22205 0.22205 0.01356 0.00917 0.1954 0.0801 "CMR10" 0.0151 nil nil 13 "CMR10" nil)
  ("Many problems required designing specific techniques or data structures for re-" 0.09729 0.90175 0.22649 0.24005 0.24005 0.01356 0.00767 0.2522 0.0575 "CMR10" 0.0151 nil nil 10 "CMR10" nil)
  ("membering and re-using results to ensure that computed properties may be" 0.09729 0.90134 0.24468 0.25823 0.25823 0.01356 0.01136 0.2352 0.1120 "CMR10" 0.0151 nil nil 10 "CMR10" nil)
  ("updated efficiently under incremental modifications to data. Recent advances" 0.09729 0.90174 0.26286 0.27641 0.27641 0.01356 0.01046 0.2390 0.0830 "CMR10" 0.0151 nil nil 8 "CMR10" nil)
  ("on self-adjusting computation (Section 10.2) offer an alternative by proposing" 0.09729 0.90191 0.28086 0.29441 0.29441 0.01356 0.00941 0.2203 0.0246 "CMR10" 0.0151 nil nil 9 "CMR10" nil)
  ("general-purpose techniques for automatically adapting computations to data" 0.09729 0.90159 0.29904 0.31259 0.31259 0.01356 0.01158 0.2247 0.1598 "CMR10" 0.0151 nil nil 7 "CMR10" nil)
  ("modifications by selectively re-executing the parts of the computation that de-" 0.09729 0.90154 0.31722 0.33078 0.33078 0.01356 0.00834 0.2654 0.1347 "CMR10" 0.0151 nil nil 10 "CMR10" nil)
  ("pend on the modifications and re-using unaffected parts. Applications of the" 0.09729 0.90149 0.33522 0.34878 0.34878 0.01356 0.01068 0.2195 0.0495 "CMR10" 0.0151 nil nil 10 "CMR10" nil)
  ("technique to problems from a reasonably diverse set of areas show that the ap-" 0.09729 0.90156 0.3534 0.36696 0.36696 0.01356 0.00788 0.2461 0.0966 "CMR10" 0.0151 nil nil 13 "CMR10" nil)
  ("proach can be effective both in theory and practice." 0.09729 0.62229 0.37159 0.38514 0.38514 0.01356 0.00778 0.2029 0.0680 "CMR10" 0.0151 nil nil 8 "CMR10" nil)
  ("We present a tutorial on a language for self-adjusting computation, called" 0.12352 0.9018 0.38959 0.40314 0.40314 0.01356 0.01059 0.2517 0.0321 "CMR10" 0.0151 nil nil 10 "CMR10" nil)
  ("ΔML (Delta ML), that extends the Standard ML (SML) language with primi-" 0.09729 0.90161 0.40777 0.42132 0.42132 0.01356 0.00865 0.3219 0.0522 "CMR10" 0.0151 nil nil 11 "CMMI10" nil)
  ("tives for self-adjusting computation." 0.09729 0.46274 0.42577 0.43932 0.43932 0.01356 0.00767 0.2705 0.0466 "CMR10" 0.0151 nil nil 3 "CMR10" nil)
  ("In self-adjusting computation, programs consist of two components: a self-" 0.12352 0.9016 0.44395 0.45751 0.45751 0.01356 0.01018 0.2621 0.0212 "CMR10" 0.0151 nil nil 9 "CMR10" nil)
  ("adjusting core and a top- or meta-level mutator. The self-adjusting core is a" 0.09729 0.90146 0.46213 0.47569 0.47569 0.01356 0.01059 0.2417 0.0934 "CMR10" 0.0151 nil nil 12 "CMR10" nil)
  ("purely functional program that performs a single run of the intended applica-" 0.09729 0.90197 0.48013 0.49369 0.49369 0.01356 0.00924 0.2419 0.0640 "CMR10" 0.0151 nil nil 11 "CMR10" nil)
  ("tion. The mutator drives the self-adjusting core by supplying the initial input" 0.09729 0.90146 0.49831 0.51187 0.51187 0.01356 0.00924 0.2466 0.0464 "CMR10" 0.0151 nil nil 11 "CMR10" nil)
  ("and by subsequently modifying data based on the application. The mutator can" 0.09729 0.90187 0.5165 0.53005 0.53005 0.01356 0.00703 0.2670 0.0374 "CMR10" 0.0151 nil nil 11 "CMR10" nil)
  ("modify the computation data in a variety of forms depending on the application." 0.09729 0.90179 0.5345 0.54805 0.54805 0.01356 0.00639 0.3042 0.0701 "CMR10" 0.0151 nil nil 12 "CMR10" nil)
  ("For example, in a physical simulation, the mutator can insert a new object into" 0.09729 0.90146 0.55268 0.56623 0.56623 0.01356 0.00752 0.2917 0.0339 "CMR10" 0.0151 nil nil 13 "CMR10" nil)
  ("the set of objects being considered. In motion simulation, the mutator changes" 0.09729 0.90192 0.57068 0.58423 0.58423 0.01356 0.00823 0.2633 0.0321 "CMR10" 0.0151 nil nil 11 "CMR10" nil)
  ("the outcomes of comparisons performed between objects as the relationship be-" 0.09729 0.90149 0.58886 0.60242 0.60242 0.01356 0.0075 0.2688 0.0321 "CMR10" 0.0151 nil nil 10 "CMR10" nil)
  ("tween objects change because of motion. After modifying computation data," 0.09729 0.90177 0.60704 0.6206 0.6206 0.01356 0.01091 0.2497 0.0580 "CMR10" 0.0151 nil nil 9 "CMR10" nil)
  ("the mutator can update the output and the computation by requesting change" 0.09729 0.90134 0.62504 0.6386 0.6386 0.01356 0.00829 0.2133 0.0321 "CMR10" 0.0151 nil nil 11 "CMR10" nil)
  ("propagation to be performed. Change propagation is at the core of self-adjusting" 0.09729 0.90164 0.64322 0.65678 0.65678 0.01356 0.00668 0.2633 0.1163 "CMR10" 0.0151 nil nil 11 "CMTI10" nil)
  ("computation: it an automatic mechanism for propagating the data modifications" 0.09729 0.90162 0.66141 0.67496 0.67496 0.01356 0.00617 0.3150 0.1340 "CMR10" 0.0151 nil nil 9 "CMR10" nil)
  ("through the computation to update the output." 0.09729 0.58088 0.67941 0.69296 0.69296 0.01356 0.00784 0.2196 0.0802 "CMR10" 0.0151 nil nil 6 "CMR10" nil)
  ("To support efficient change propagation, we represent a computation with a" 0.12352 0.90157 0.69759 0.71114 0.71114 0.01356 0.0084 0.2446 0.0264 "CMR10" 0.0151 nil nil 10 "CMR10" nil)
  ("trace that records the data and control dependences in the computation. Change" 0.09729 0.90216 0.71559 0.72914 0.72914 0.01356 0.00634 0.2572 0.0483 "CMR10" 0.0151 nil nil 11 "CMTI10" nil)
  ("propagation uses the trace to identify and re-execute the parts of the compu-" 0.09729 0.90166 0.73377 0.74733 0.74733 0.01356 0.00946 0.1932 0.1216 "CMR10" 0.0151 nil nil 12 "CMR10" nil)
  ("tation that depend on the modified data while re-using the parts unaffected" 0.09729 0.90146 0.75195 0.76551 0.76551 0.01356 0.01085 0.2010 0.0607 "CMR10" 0.0151 nil nil 11 "CMR10" nil)
  ("by the changes. The structure and the representation of the trace is critical to" 0.09729 0.90144 0.76995 0.78351 0.78351 0.01356 0.0084 0.2000 0.0245 "CMR10" 0.0151 nil nil 13 "CMR10" nil)
  ("the effectiveness of the change propagation. Techniques have been developed for" 0.09729 0.9015 0.78814 0.80169 0.80169 0.01356 0.00701 0.2226 0.0321 "CMR10" 0.0151 nil nil 10 "CMR10" nil)
  ("implementing both tracing and change propagation efficiently (Section 10.2)." 0.09729 0.87688 0.80632 0.81987 0.81987 0.01356 0.00777 0.2668 0.1370 "CMR10" 0.0151 nil nil 8 "CMR10" nil)
  ("The ΔML language provides linguistic facilities for writing self-adjusting pro-" 0.12352 0.90196 0.82432 0.83787 0.83787 0.01356 0.00701 0.3117 0.0399 "CMR10" 0.0151 nil nil 9 "CMR10" nil)
  ("grams consisting of a core and a mutator. To this end, the language distinguishes" 0.09729 0.90164 0.8425 0.85605 0.85605 0.01356 0.006 0.2979 0.0610 "CMR10" 0.0151 nil nil 13 "CMR10" nil)
  ("between two kinds of function spaces: conventional and self-adjusting. The mu-" 0.09729 0.90155 0.8605 0.87406 0.87406 0.01356 0.00778 0.2649 0.0817 "CMR10" 0.0151 nil nil 10 "CMR10" nil)
  ("tator consists solely of conventional functions. The self-adjusting core consists" 0.09729 0.90178 0.87868 0.89224 0.89224 0.01356 0.00924 0.2171 0.0504 "CMR10" 0.0151 nil nil 9 "CMR10" nil))
  (17
  ("Self-adjusting Computation with Delta ML" 0.41906 0.82455 0.04354 0.05574 0.05574 0.0122 0.00735 0.3379 0.1273 "CMR9" 0.0136 nil nil 4 "CMR9" nil)
  ("3" 0.8907 0.90141 0.04354 0.05574 0.05574 0.0122 nil nil 0.0107 "CMR9" 0.0136 nil nil 0 "CMR9" nil)
  ("of self-adjusting functions and all pure (self-adjusting or conventional) functions" 0.09729 0.90181 0.08158 0.09514 0.09514 0.01356 0.00673 0.2522 0.0188 "CMR10" 0.0151 nil nil 9 "CMR10" nil)
  ("that they call directly or indirectly (transitively)." 0.09729 0.59726 0.09977 0.11332 0.11332 0.01356 0.00767 0.2286 0.0426 "CMR10" 0.0151 nil nil 6 "CMR10" nil)
  ("ΔML enables the programmer to mark the computation data that is expected" 0.12352 0.90155 0.11795 0.1315 0.1315 0.01356 0.00655 0.3164 0.0522 "CMR10" 0.0151 nil nil 11 "CMMI10" nil)
  ("to change across runs (or over time) by placing them into modifiable references" 0.09729 0.90141 0.13595 0.1495 0.1495 0.01356 0.00816 0.2776 0.0205 "CMR10" 0.0151 nil nil 12 "CMR10" nil)
  ("or modifiables for short. For implementing a self-adjusting core, ΔML provides" 0.09729 0.90181 0.15413 0.16769 0.16769 0.01356 0.00879 0.3229 0.0208 "CMR10" 0.0151 nil nil 10 "CMR10" nil)
  ("facilities for creating and reading modifiables within a self-adjusting function. In" 0.09729 0.90183 0.17231 0.18587 0.18587 0.01356 0.00655 0.2926 0.0831 "CMR10" 0.0151 nil nil 10 "CMR10" nil)
  ("this tutorial, we do not include the update operation on modifiables in the core—" 0.09729 0.90211 0.19031 0.20387 0.20387 0.01356 0.00583 0.3001 0.0374 "CMR10" 0.0151 nil nil 13 "CMR10" nil)
  ("modifiables are write-once within the self-adjusting core.^{1} ΔML also provides" 0.09729 0.90179 0.20618 0.22205 0.22205 0.01356 0.01007 0.2776 0.1141 "CMR10" 0.0151 nil nil 9 "CMR10" nil)
  ("facilities for defining self-adjusting functions to be memoized if so desired. For" 0.09729 0.90157 0.22649 0.24005 0.24005 0.01356 0.00879 0.2672 0.0832 "CMR10" 0.0151 nil nil 11 "CMR10" nil)
  ("implementing a mutator, ΔML provides meta-level facilities to create, read, and" 0.09729 0.90161 0.24468 0.25823 0.25823 0.01356 0.00698 0.3389 0.1369 "CMR10" 0.0151 nil nil 10 "CMR10" nil)
  ("update modifiables, and to perform change propagation. The mutator can use the" 0.09729 0.90145 0.26286 0.27641 0.27641 0.01356 0.00527 0.3162 0.0700 "CMR10" 0.0151 nil nil 11 "CMR10" nil)
  ("update operation to modify destructively the contents of modifiables—this is how" 0.09729 0.9016 0.28086 0.29441 0.29441 0.01356 0.00533 0.3343 0.0701 "CMR10" 0.0151 nil nil 10 "CMR10" nil)
  ("mutators modify the inputs of the self-adjusting core. After such modifications" 0.09729 0.90154 0.29904 0.3126 0.3126 0.01356 0.0084 0.2782 0.0913 "CMR10" 0.0151 nil nil 10 "CMR10" nil)
  ("are performed, the mutator can use change propagation to update the result of" 0.09729 0.9018 0.31722 0.33078 0.33078 0.01356 0.0078 0.2312 0.0312 "CMR10" 0.0151 nil nil 12 "CMR10" nil)
  ("the core." 0.09729 0.18525 0.33522 0.34878 0.34878 0.01356 0.00784 0.1448 0.0321 "CMR10" 0.0151 nil nil 1 "CMR10" nil)
  ("Writing a self-adjusting program is very similar to writing a conventional," 0.12352 0.90229 0.3534 0.36696 0.36696 0.01356 0.01068 0.2844 0.0773 "CMR10" 0.0151 nil nil 10 "CMR10" nil)
  ("purely functional program. Using the techniques described in this tutorial, it" 0.09729 0.90156 0.37159 0.38514 0.38514 0.01356 0.01031 0.2457 0.0639 "CMR10" 0.0151 nil nil 10 "CMR10" nil)
  ("is not hard to take an existing purely functional SML program and make it" 0.09729 0.90148 0.38959 0.40314 0.40314 0.01356 0.01059 0.2434 0.0155 "CMR10" 0.0151 nil nil 13 "CMR10" nil)
  ("self-adjusting by annotating the code with ΔML primitives. Annotated code is" 0.09729 0.90149 0.40777 0.42132 0.42132 0.01356 0.00851 0.2861 0.1340 "CMR10" 0.0151 nil nil 10 "CMR10" nil)
  ("guaranteed to respond to modifications to its data correctly: the result of an" 0.09729 0.90179 0.42577 0.43932 0.43932 0.01356 0.01012 0.1983 0.1119 "CMR10" 0.0151 nil nil 12 "CMR10" nil)
  ("updated run is equivalent to a (from-scratch) run. Guaranteeing efficient change" 0.09729 0.90185 0.44395 0.45751 0.45751 0.01356 0.00668 0.2741 0.0830 "CMR10" 0.0151 nil nil 10 "CMR10" nil)
  ("propagation, however, may require some additional effort: we sometimes need to" 0.09729 0.90147 0.46213 0.47569 0.47569 0.01356 0.00647 0.3111 0.1280 "CMR10" 0.0151 nil nil 10 "CMR10" nil)
  ("modify the algorithm or use a different algorithm to achieve the optimal update" 0.09729 0.90139 0.48013 0.49369 0.49369 0.01356 0.00701 0.3031 0.0700 "CMR10" 0.0151 nil nil 12 "CMR10" nil)
  ("times." 0.09729 0.15785 0.49831 0.51187 0.51187 0.01356 nil 0.4073 0.0606 "CMR10" 0.0151 nil nil 0 "CMR10" nil)
  ("When an algorithm does not yield to efficient change propagation, it is some-" 0.12352 0.90166 0.5165 0.53005 0.53005 0.01356 0.00711 0.3199 0.0598 "CMR10" 0.0151 nil nil 12 "CMR10" nil)
  ("times possible to change it slightly to regain efficiency, often by eliminating" 0.09729 0.90145 0.5345 0.54805 0.54805 0.01356 0.0113 0.2671 0.0541 "CMR10" 0.0151 nil nil 11 "CMR10" nil)
  ("unnecessary dependences between computation data and control. For example," 0.09729 0.9018 0.55268 0.56623 0.56623 0.01356 0.00823 0.2254 0.1210 "CMR10" 0.0151 nil nil 8 "CMR10" nil)
  ("the effectiveness of a self-adjusting mergesort algorithm can be improved by em-" 0.09729 0.90153 0.57068 0.58423 0.58423 0.01356 0.0066 0.3011 0.0322 "CMR10" 0.0151 nil nil 11 "CMR10" nil)
  ("ploying a divide-and-conquer strategy that divides the input into two sublists" 0.09729 0.90161 0.58886 0.60242 0.60242 0.01356 0.00941 0.2158 0.0736 "CMR10" 0.0151 nil nil 10 "CMR10" nil)
